# A rate law over a MULTI-PATTERN Species observable must not full-walk
# the pool every event (issue #81).
#
# #79 / #80 put a single-pattern `Species` observable on the incremental
# tracker and flushed its dirty complexes after every event.  A
# multi-pattern one was left behind: `init_incremental_observables`
# admitted a `Species` observable only when it declared exactly one
# pattern, so this model fell through to
# `compute_rate_dependent_observables` and paid the same O(pool) walk per
# event that #79 measured.
#
# `Rtot` carries four patterns, two of which overlap: every R matches
# both `R()` and exactly one of `R(x~U)` / `R(x~P)`.  Overlap is the
# point — the full walk in `evaluate_observable` re-walks the pool once
# per pattern and adds one for every complex that pattern passes on its
# own, so a complex two patterns match counts twice.  Every R and S here
# is a free monomer, so each pattern's species count is that pattern's
# molecule count, which makes multi_pattern_molecules_obs_model.bngl —
# identical but for the one word `Species` — both the cost floor and an
# exact trajectory oracle.
#
# Paired with: tests/cpp/multi_pattern_species_obs_test.cpp

begin model
begin parameters
    R_tot   30000
    S_tot   10000
    kp      0.05
    kt      1e-6
end parameters

begin molecule types
    R(x~U~P)
    S()
    P()
end molecule types

begin seed species
    R(x~U)   R_tot
    S()      S_tot
    P()      0
end seed species

begin observables
    Species  Rtot  R(x~U), R(x~P), R(), S()
end observables

begin functions
    conv_rate() = kt*Rtot
end functions

begin reaction rules
    R(x~U) -> R(x~P)   kp
    R() -> P()         conv_rate()
end reaction rules
end model

begin actions
simulate({method=>"nf",t_end=>10,n_steps=>10,print_functions=>1})
end actions
